<DocsLayout title="LangGraph guide | dotfetch" description="Integrate dotfetch with LangGraph: per-turn tool selection as create_agent middleware, approval through interrupt(), readable tool errors and run context.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Framework guide</p>
  <h1>LangGraph</h1>
  <p class="lead">dotfetch plugs into LangGraph's own mechanisms: a <code>create_agent</code> middleware that picks each model turn's tools, <code>interrupt()</code> for human approval, and the graph's runtime for your context. Failures come back to the model as error <code>ToolMessage</code>s instead of ending the run.</p>
  <p><a class="pill" href="/dotfetch/docs/frameworks/langgraph/advanced/">Advanced LangGraph usage →</a></p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`uv add "dotfetch[langgraph]"   # langchain, langchain-core, langgraph >= 1.0`} />

  <h2>Basic page: per-turn tools</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Register tools with <code>@fetcher.register</code> and <code>await fetcher.sync()</code>.</li>
    <li>Add <code>await adapter.middleware()</code> to <code>create_agent</code>. It brings the dotfetch tools with it.</li>
    <li>Run the agent normally. Each model turn sees only the tools dotfetch selects for the newest message or tool result.</li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" title="Per-turn tool selection" code={`import asyncio

from langchain.agents import create_agent
from dotfetch import DotFetch
from dotfetch.langgraph import LangGraphAdapter

fetcher = DotFetch()

@fetcher.register(examples=["where is my order", "has order 4471 shipped"])
def get_order_status(order_id: str) -> str:
    """Return the current shipping status for an order."""
    return "in_transit"

@fetcher.register(examples=["refund order 4471"], requires_confirmation=True)
def refund_order(order_id: str) -> str:
    """Refund an order in full."""
    return "refunded"

async def main():
    await fetcher.sync()
    adapter = LangGraphAdapter(fetcher)
    agent = create_agent("openai:gpt-5.5", middleware=[await adapter.middleware(limit=5)])
    result = await agent.ainvoke({"messages": [("user", "Has order 4471 shipped?")]})
    print(result["messages"][-1].content)

asyncio.run(main())`} />
  <p>Tools you pass to <code>create_agent(tools=...)</code> yourself stay available every turn; only the dotfetch tools are narrowed. <code>await adapter.areact_agent(model, limit=5)</code> builds the same agent in one call.</p>

  <h2>What the model sees when a tool fails</h2>
  <p>A failed call raises LangChain's <code>ToolException</code>, and dotfetch's tools set <code>handle_tool_error=True</code>, so the model receives an error <code>ToolMessage</code> (<code>status="error"</code>) with <code>&lt;type&gt;: &lt;message&gt;</code>, capped at 2,000 characters. It can correct a bad argument or try something else.</p>
  <div class="callout"><strong>Redact or opt out:</strong> <code>LangGraphAdapter(fetcher, error_formatter=redact)</code> rewrites the text the model sees; <code>error_mode="raise"</code> lets <code>ToolExecutionError</code> propagate instead.</div>

  <h2>Reading your context from a dotfetch tool</h2>
  <p>Whatever you pass as the graph's <code>context=</code> is available while the tool runs, without changing its schema.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`from dotfetch.langgraph import current_tool_runtime

@fetcher.register
async def get_balance(account: str) -> str:
    """Fetch an account balance for the signed-in user."""
    runtime = current_tool_runtime()   # None outside a run
    return await runtime.context.bank.balance(account)

# agent = create_agent(model, middleware=[...], context_schema=AppContext)
# await agent.ainvoke({"messages": [...]}, context=AppContext(bank=bank))`} />
</DocsLayout>
